WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.360
Least privilege means granting only the access needed to perform a specific job.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.340
In cloud IAM, this is easy to say and harder to operate.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.760
Teams often begin with broad administrator, owner, contributor, wildcard,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.180
or full-access permissions because they make work move quickly.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:23.080
The risk is that those permissions can also create resources, modify policy, expose data,

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:28.900
change networks, disable logging, or delete systems far beyond the original task.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.320
A practical least-privilege process starts with tasks.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.300
What does this person, pipeline, function, or service need to do?

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.200
Which actions are required?

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.940
Which resources are in scope?

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.040
Does the work apply only to development, production, one account, one project,

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.540
one storage bucket, one key, or one application?

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.640
Can conditions limit access by tag, environment, source, approval state, or time?

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:58.420
The more specific the task, the easier it is to design narrow permissions.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.200
Separation of duties is part of least privilege.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.740
The same identity should not casually be able to develop code, approve deployment,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.680
change logging, modify security controls, and grant itself more access.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.620
In real environments, duties sometimes overlap, especially in small teams,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.600
but the risk should be visible.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:23.100
Sensitive actions such as changing trust relationships, attaching policies, creating keys,

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.240
or modifying deployment automation deserve extra review and monitoring.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.140
Least privilege is an engineering process, not a one-time setting.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.560
Permissions need to be tested, monitored, reviewed, and adjusted as applications change.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.020
Overly tight access can block work and lead to emergency grants.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.800
Overly broad access increases risk.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.340
The healthy middle is iterative: start from known tasks, scope carefully,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.240
collect access-denied feedback, approve changes deliberately,

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.100
and remove permissions that are no longer needed.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.640
Least privilege also benefits reliability.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.140
When an automation role can only change the resources it owns,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.200
accidental deployments are less likely to damage unrelated systems.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.340
When a support role can view diagnostics without editing production policy,

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.400
troubleshooting becomes safer.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.620
Narrow permissions reduce both security risk and operational blast radius.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.920
Access-denied events can be useful feedback when handled carefully.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.740
They may show that a role is too narrow for a legitimate task,

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.800
or they may reveal that a person or workload is attempting something unexpected.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.740
Mature teams use those signals to refine roles rather than reflexively granting broad

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:33.920
access.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.740
Iteration is normal; unmanaged broadening is the danger.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:42.760
One practical pattern is to create task-oriented roles instead of person-oriented

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.260
privilege piles.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.000
A database support role, deployment role, log reader role,

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.660
or incident response role can be reviewed against a specific job.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.520
If the job changes, the role changes.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.540
If a person changes teams, membership changes.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.160
This makes access easier to reason about than broad grants attached directly to

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.420
individuals.
